Let the one-step source increments be $$l_j=\int_0^1S(1-s)f^\#(j+s)\,\mathrm{d}s,\qquad j\in\mathbb{Z}.$$ Then $\|l_j\|_E\le CMq^j$. Define $$\label{flat:recursion-solution}
 v_j=\sum_{k=0}^\infty P_s^k\Pi_s l_{j-1-k}
       -\sum_{k=0}^\infty P_u^{-k-1}\Pi_u l_{j+k}.$$ Both series converge absolutely for every integer $j$. More explicitly, their norms are at most $$CMq^j\left[
   q^{-1}\sum_{k=0}^\infty(r_s/q)^k
       +r_u^{-1}\sum_{k=0}^\infty(q/r_u)^k\right]
 \le C'Mq^j.$$ Shifting the summation indices proves $v_{j+1}=Pv_j+l_j$. On $[j,j+1]$ define $v$ by <a href="#flat:duhamel" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="flat:duhamel">[flat:duhamel]</a> with initial value $v_j$. The definitions agree at the endpoints and give $$\label{flat:preliminary-bound}
 \|v(\tau)\|_E\le CM e^{-\gamma\tau},\qquad
 |V(z,t)|\le CM\rho^{-1+\delta}.$$ The continuous joining creates no distributional time-boundary term, so <a href="#flat:similarity-equation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="flat:similarity-equation">[flat:similarity-equation]</a> holds across every joined face. Interior regularity makes it a classical equation there. The construction is linear and real on real sources. The second sum in <a href="#flat:recursion-solution" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="flat:recursion-solution">[flat:recursion-solution]</a> selects the outside spectral components using future forcing; its use is permitted precisely because no initial values have been imposed.

