This identification is a bijection between $C^\infty[0,1]$ and the smooth invariant functions on $\Sigma$. The pushforward of spherical volume under $x$ is a positive constant multiple of $wv_*^2\,\mathrm{d}x$. With the nonpositive Laplacian convention, put $L=\frac14\Delta_\Sigma$. On invariant functions, $$\label{geo:sphere-operator}
 L=x(1-x)\partial_x^2+\big(n+2-(n+5)x\big)\partial_x,
 \qquad
 \mathcal A=(-L+n+3)(-L+2(n+2)).$$ The right-hand expression defines a smooth elliptic operator of order four on all of $\Sigma$.*



*In the core coordinates $(z,\theta)\mapsto(z,\sqrt{1-|z|^2}\,\theta)$, with $\theta\in S^5$, its Laplacian on functions independent of $\theta$ is $$\label{geo:local-laplacian}
 \Delta_\Sigma
 =\Delta_z-\sum_{i,j=1}^m z_i z_j\partial_{z_i z_j}
             -(m+5)\sum_{i=1}^m z_i\partial_{z_i}.$$ The flat momentum operator obtained by replacing $v_*$ by $x$ in <a href="#geo:momentum" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:momentum">[geo:momentum]</a> satisfies $$\label{geo:flat-operator}
 \widehat\mathcal AH
 :=\big[x^{1-n}(x^{n+1}H)''\big]''
 =\big(x\partial_x^2+(n+2)\partial_x\big)^2H
 =\frac1{16}\Delta_{\mathbb{R}^m}^2H,\qquad x=|z|^2.$$

Let $G\in C^\infty[0,\infty)$ satisfy $G(0)=1$ and $G'(0)\ne0$, and define $F$ by <a href="#geo:selfsimilar" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:selfsimilar">[geo:selfsimilar]</a>. Suppose that for some $T>0$ and $\delta>0$ there is a smooth invariant solution $H=F+V$ of <a href="#geo:equation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:equation">[geo:equation]</a> on $(-T,0)\times\Sigma$ such that $1+v_*H>0$ and $$\label{geo:correction-bound}
 V(t,0)=O(\rho^{-1+\delta})\qquad(t\uparrow0).$$ Then a smooth $U(10)$-invariant metric $\omega_0\in[\omega_*]$ has maximal smooth Calabi-flow existence interval $[0,T_*)$, where $T_*=T/2<\infty$. At the fixed affine origin $p$, the scalar curvature of this flow satisfies $$\label{geo:curvature-rate}
 S(\tau,p)=a(T_*-\tau)^{-1/2}
       +O\big(1+(T_*-\tau)^{(-1+\delta)/2}\big),\qquad
 a=-110G'(0)\ne0.$$ In particular $S(\tau,p)\sim a(T_*-\tau)^{-1/2}$, and $a>0$ when $G'(0)<0$.

There exist a constant $c>0$ and a function $G\in C^\infty([0,\infty))$ such that $$\label{prof:profile-equation}
 Q(y\partial_y)(G-1)=\frac{y^2}{2}\left(\frac1G-\frac1c\right),
 \qquad Q(D)=(D-1)(D+9)(D+10).$$ The function $G$ is bounded above and bounded away from zero, satisfies $G(0)=1$ and $G'(0)<0$, and is analytic near zero. There are real coefficients $g_j$, $j\ge1$, such that, for every $J,\ell\ge0$, $$\label{prof:expansion}
 \frac{\mathrm{d}^\ell}{\mathrm{d}y^\ell}
 \left(G(y)-c-\sum_{j=1}^{J}g_jy^{-2j}\right)
 =O\bigl(y^{-2(J+1)-\ell}\bigr)\qquad(y\longrightarrow\infty).$$ In particular, $u\mapsto G(u^{-1/2})$ extends smoothly to $u=0$ from the right, with value $c$.*


*Set $h(y)=(G(y)-1)/y$, with its smooth value at zero. For $t<0$, the function $$\label{prof:flat-shrinker}
 F(t,x)=\rho^{-1}h(x/\rho),\qquad \rho=\sqrt{-t},$$ solves $$F_t+(1+xF)^2\widehat{\mathcal A}F=0,
 \qquad \widehat{\mathcal A}=(x\partial_x^2+12\partial_x)^2.$$

For $|k-k_0|\le .1$ and $|d-d_0|\le .1$ in $\mathbb{C}$, there is a unique solution germ of <a href="#prof:ode" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:ode">[prof:ode]</a>, analytic in $r=s^{3/2}$, of the form $$\label{prof:origin-series}
 p=d+kr+\sum_{i\ge2}a_ir^i,\qquad r=s^{3/2}.$$ The series is jointly holomorphic in $r,k,d$ for $|r|<3$ and satisfies $$\label{prof:origin-majorant}
 3|k|+\sum_{i\ge2}|a_i|3^i<.8.$$ Each of $p(1),p'(1),p''(1)$ has modulus at most $2$ on this parameter polydisc. For the real parameters in <a href="#prof:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:parameters">[prof:parameters]</a>, $1+p>0$ on $0\le s\le1$.

The roots in <a href="#prof:coordinates" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:coordinates">[prof:coordinates]</a> are distinct and satisfy $$\label{prof:root-boxes}
 \begin{split}
 -1.08659&<\lambda_0<-1.08655,\\
 .40933&<\Re\lambda_1<.40938,\qquad
 .8671<\Im\lambda_1<.86721.
 \end{split}$$ Every entry of $\Gamma-\Lambda$ has modulus less than $.001$. The absolute row sums of $\Gamma$ are less than $.92,.68,.68$, respectively. With $$\label{prof:convolution-constants}
 M_i=\sum_{j=0}^2
 \frac{|\lambda_j|^i}{|\Re\lambda_j|\,|D_0'(\lambda_j)|},\qquad 0\le i\le2,$$ one has $$M_0<1.96,\qquad M_1<1.92,\qquad M_2<1.88,
 \qquad \frac1{\Re\lambda_1|D_0'(\lambda_1)|}<.82.$$

Let $p$ be the matched solution above. There are uniquely determined coefficients $d_j$, extending <a href="#prof:asymptotic-polynomial" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:asymptotic-polynomial">[prof:asymptotic-polynomial]</a>, such that for every $J,\ell\ge0$, $$\label{prof:s-expansion}
 \frac{\mathrm{d}^\ell}{\mathrm{d}s^\ell}
 \left(p(s)-\sum_{j=1}^{J}d_js^{-3j}\right)
 =O\bigl(s^{-3(J+1)-\ell}\bigr).$$

Under these assumptions the following estimates hold.*

1.  *If $Q_R=B_R\times(-R^4,0]$ and $\mathcal L u=f$ on $Q_2$, then $$\label{ana:interior-schauder}
     \mathcal N_\alpha(u;Q_1)
     \le C\bigl(|f|_{\alpha;Q_2}+\|u\|_{\infty;Q_2}\bigr).$$*


2.  *If $0<h\le1$, $\mathcal L u=f$ on $B_2\times[0,h]$, and $u(\cdot,0)=0$, then $$\label{ana:initial-schauder}
     \mathcal N_\alpha(u;B_1\times[0,h])
     \le C\bigl(|f|_{\alpha;B_2\times[0,h]}
                       +\|u\|_{\infty;B_2\times[0,h]}\bigr).$$ Here $C$ depends only on $d,\alpha,\lambda,K$ and the fixed spatial gaps, and in <span class="upright">(ii)</span> is independent of $h$.*

*These are a priori estimates for classical solutions in the indicated spaces; smooth approximation gives the same conclusions for limits of such solutions. The usual interior regularity theorem also applies: with smooth coefficients, distributional solutions in $L^2_{\rm loc}$ and Hölder sources are locally in the space <a href="#ana:local-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ana:local-norm">[ana:local-norm]</a>, and are smooth when the source is smooth. The estimates are from preceding times and require no terminal data.

Let $U_1\Subset U_2$ be fixed bounded spatial domains and $b_0<b_1<b$, with $b-b_1$ bounded above. Suppose that the coefficient assumptions of Lemma <a href="#ana:schauder" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="ana:schauder">13</a> hold on $U_2\times(b_0,b)$ and that $\mathcal Lu=f$ there. Then $$\label{ana:l2-estimate}
 \mathcal N_\alpha(u;U_1\times[b_1,b))
 \le C\bigl(|f|_{\alpha;U_2\times(b_0,b)}
                  +\|u\|_{L^2(U_2\times(b_0,b))}\bigr).$$ The constant depends on the spatial gap, $b_1-b_0$, the upper bound for $b-b_1$, and the coefficient constants, but requires no terminal trace. The estimate is unchanged if the upper face is included and the solution is defined there. In particular it holds on fixed nested annuli through an open upper time endpoint.

Under the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#ana:short" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="ana:short">15</a>, the local scaled parabolic $C^\alpha$ norms of the spatial derivatives of order $0\le j\le3$, with weight $p-j$, satisfy $$\label{ana:lower-small}
 \sup_P\bigl|D_y^j(s_0^{-p}u\circ\Psi_P)\bigr|_{\alpha;D_P}
 \le C\eta^{(4-j-\alpha)/4}\|f\|_{Y_{\alpha,p}(s)}.$$ If the coefficient and source bounds also hold at an exponent $\alpha'\in(\alpha,1)$, then $$\label{ana:fourth-small}
 \|u\|_{X'_{\alpha,p}(s)}
 \le C\eta^{(\alpha'-\alpha)/4}
                    \|f\|_{Y_{\alpha',p}(s)}.$$ Constants may depend on $\alpha'$ but remain independent of the scale and elapsed time.

Let $G$ be the profile of Theorem <a href="#prof:profile" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prof:profile">6</a>, put $$h(y)=\frac{G(y)-1}{y},\qquad
 F(t,x)=\rho^{-1}h(x/\rho),$$ and fix $0<\alpha<\alpha'<1$. Choose $\delta\in(0,1)$ and the ancient right inverse supplied by Theorem <a href="#flat:inverse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="flat:inverse">18</a>. For every sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$, there are $T>0$ and a real invariant function $V\in X_\alpha$ on $\Sigma\times[-T,0)$ such that $\lVert V\rVert_{X'_\alpha}\le\varepsilon$ and $H=F+V$ satisfies $$\label{glue:equation}
 H_t+(1+v_*H)^2\mathcal AH=0,\qquad v_*=x(1-x).$$ The function $H$ is smooth on $\Sigma\times(-T,0)$, and there are constants $0<c_1<c_2<\infty$, independent of $t$, such that $$\label{glue:positive}
 c_1\le 1+v_*H\le c_2.$$ In particular, $H(t,\cdot)$ is smooth as a function of $x\in[0,1]$, and $$\label{glue:center}
 V(t,0)=O(\rho^{-1+\delta})\qquad(t\uparrow0).$$

For $0<T\le1$, on the compact domain one has $$\label{glue:background-bounds}
 F=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(b^{-2}),\qquad
 \mathcal AF=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(b^{-6}),\qquad
 A_c=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(1),\qquad
 B_c=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(b^{-4}),\qquad
 v_*=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(b^2).$$ The analogous flat bounds hold in a fixed neighborhood of $r=0$. In the common coordinates there, write $$\mathcal P-\widehat\mathcal P=\sum_{|\nu|\le4}d_\nu(z,t)\partial_z^\nu.$$ For each $j,k\ge0$, the rescaled coefficients satisfy $$\label{glue:mismatch-coeff}
 b^{4-|\nu|+j+4k}
 \lvert \partial_z^j\partial_t^k d_\nu\rvert\le C_{j,k}b^2.$$ Finally, near $r=0$, $$\label{glue:error-order}
 E_c=O_{\mathrm{sc}}(b^{-4}).$$ Away from a fixed neighborhood of $r=0$, all these background quantities extend smoothly to $t=0$ with bounds of every finite order.

For the right inverses of Proposition <a href="#glue:inverse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="glue:inverse">22</a>, $$\label{glue:DE-small}
 \lVert DE_c\rVert_{X'_\alpha}
 \le C\left(\beta^{2-2\delta}
       +\eta^{(\alpha'-\alpha)/4}
       +\eta^{(1-\alpha)/4}\right).$$ In particular this norm becomes arbitrarily small by choosing $\beta$ and then $\eta=T/\beta^4$ sufficiently small.

For every parameter $z_*\in[-R,R]^2$, where $R=5\cdot10^{-10}$, let $(k,d)$ be given by <a href="#prof:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:parameters">[prof:parameters]</a>, and let $p$ be the origin solution of <a href="#prof:ode" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:ode">[prof:ode]</a> supplied by Lemma <a href="#prof:origin" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prof:origin">7</a>. This solution extends to $0\le s\le S_0=56$ and satisfies $$1+p(s)>0.$$ For the coordinates of its jet relative to $p_{\rm a}$, defined in <a href="#prof:coordinates" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="prof:coordinates">[prof:coordinates]</a>, one has $$\label{cert:conclusion}
 |q|\le .25R,\qquad
 \big\|(\Re\xi,\Im\xi)-z_*\big\|_\infty\le .13R.$$
